detail.abstract:
Extreme weather events are increasingly common, yet it remains unclear whether direct experience with them produces lasting shifts in climate attitudes. Consistent with experiential-learning accounts, we hypothesize that exposure initially raises climate and broader environmental concern, but that this effect fades as the event recedes from memory. Using novel longitudinal data from Australian respondents surveyed repeatedly since the catastrophic 2019-2020 Black Summer bushfires, we track attitudes well beyond the disaster's immediate aftermath. We find that bushfire exposure is associated with significantly higher climate concern and dissatisfaction with national environmental policy. Strikingly, these differences persist for several years. However, they dissipate over time, demonstrating short to medium durability rather than permanent shifts.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Paper presented for the annual convention of the American Political Science Association, Boston, USA, September 3-6, 2026.&nbsp;
</div>
